🔍 Abstraction vs Encapsulation
| Concept                | Abstraction 🧠                                                             | Encapsulation 🔐                                                         |
| ---------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **Definition**         | Hiding **implementation details**, showing only the **essential features** | Hiding **internal state/data** and **restricting access** to it          |
| **Purpose**            | Focus on **what** an object does                                           | Control **how** data is accessed/modified                                |
| **Achieved by**        | Abstract classes, interfaces                                               | `private`, `protected` fields + getters/setters                          |
| **Used for**           | Designing the **architecture** or blueprint                                | **Securing and protecting** object’s data                                |
| **Example in Java**    | Interface with `send()` method but no implementation                       | Private fields in a class with public getters/setters                    |
| **Real-World Analogy** | You **drive a car** without knowing how the engine works                   | You **can’t directly open the engine**, only use buttons/controls safely |


✅ Examples
🔷 Abstraction (focus on what)
java
Copy
Edit
interface Payment {
    void pay(double amount); // Abstract method (no implementation)
}

class CreditCard implements Payment {
    public void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid " + amount + " using credit card");
    }
}
You know how to use pay(), but not how it's implemented.

🔐 Encapsulation (focus on data protection)
java
Copy
Edit
class BankAccount {
    private double balance; // Internal data is hidden

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0) balance += amount;
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}
balance is protected. You can't directly access it, only through methods.

🧠 In One Line:
Abstraction is about hiding complexity,
Encapsulation is about hiding data.

